<%# Shared body for all three approval outcome emails. The key path comes from
    @copy_scope, set by Spree::CustomerMailer#deliver_outcome, so it is defined once in
    the mailer rather than repeated in three templates.

    ── shaped by his actual file, not by a guess ────────────────────────────────

    Every key below is one he wrote. Read out of business.en.yml:

      account_approval:  subject heading greeting body body_txt login_details action
                         portal_note what_next_title what_bullets what_bullets_txt
                         thank_you
      account_not_approved:
                         subject heading greeting body body_txt contact contact_txt
                         thank_you
                         (nested under account_approval until 19 Aug; he moved it up)

    So the two sections overlap but do not match: the approved one has a button and a
    bullet list, the other has a contact paragraph. Everything except the heading is
    therefore optional and a missing key renders nothing at all rather than a
    translation_missing span. The mailer refuses to send while the heading is blank, so
    an empty template cannot go out.

    note: and contact_url: are passed to EVERY lookup. His approved body uses %{note} and
    his fourth bullet uses %{contact_url}; I18n ignores an argument a string does not use
    and raises only on one it wants. Passing both means he can move either between
    sections without a code change.
%>
<%# The shop's own common values, passed to every lookup for the same reason
    note: and contact_url: are. His Canadian welcome copy uses %{shop_url}, his
    "what next" bullets use %{shop_url_full} and %{support_url}, and I18n raises
    on an argument a string wants and was not given.

    That is not hypothetical. The welcome email for Canada raised

      missing interpolation argument :shop_url in
      "We're excited to have you join us at <strong>%{shop_url}</strong>..."

    for every new customer, and only on Canada, because Canada is the one shop
    whose locale file overrides that key with his own wording. Nothing rendered
    it until an email smoke test was written, so it had never once been built.

    Read from his own user_emails.common section, falling back to the store's own
    url so a shop that has not written those keys still sends. %>
<% common = ->(key, fallback) { Spree.t(key, scope: 'user_emails.common', default: fallback) } %>
<% store_host = @current_store&.url.to_s.sub(%r{\Ahttps?://}, '') %>
<% shop_args = {
     shop_url: common.call(:shop_url, store_host),
     shop_url_full: common.call(:shop_url_full, "https://#{store_host}"),
     support_url: common.call(:support_url, @contact_url.to_s)
   } %>
<% t = ->(key, **args) {
     Spree.t(key, scope: @copy_scope, note: @note, contact_url: @contact_url,
             default: '', **shop_args, **args)
   } %>

<%= render layout: 'spree/shared/mailer_hero' do %>
  <%# His two files name the same three roles differently, and both are his:
  #
  #     role      commercial file   retail files
  #     heading   heading           welcome_message
  #     greeting  greeting          welcome_customer
  #     lead      -                 welcome_family
  #
  # Read either, prefer whichever this shop actually wrote. Before this, the
  # retail shops matched none of the names on the left, so their heading and
  # greeting came from the commercial file that happened to be loaded beside
  # them, and five keys they DID write were never rendered by anything. %>
  <h1><%= t.call(:heading).presence || t.call(:welcome_message) %></h1>

  <% name = @user.first_name.presence || @user.email %>
  <% greeting = t.call(:greeting, name: name).presence || t.call(:welcome_customer, name: name) %>
  <% if greeting.present? %>
    <p class="greeting"><%= greeting %></p>
  <% end %>

  <% lead = t.call(:welcome_family) %>
  <% if lead.present? %>
    <p class="lead"><%= raw lead %></p>
  <% end %>

  <%# raw, because his copy carries <strong> and <a> around the words that matter. The
      file is one only he and we can write. %>
  <% body = t.call(:body) %>
  <% if body.present? %>
    <p><%= raw body %></p>
  <% end %>

  <%# The safety net for the thing that matters most. If his wording ever stops placing
      %{note}, the shop's typed request would silently never reach the customer, which is
      the entire point of the email. Rendered here only when his copy has not carried it. %>
  <% if @note.present? && !body.to_s.include?(@note) %>
    <p style="margin:16px 0;padding:12px 16px;background:#f6f7f9;border-left:3px solid #d0d5dd;">
      <%= simple_format(@note) %>
    </p>
  <% end %>

  <% login_details = t.call(:login_details, email: @user.email) %>
  <% if login_details.present? %>
    <p class="sub"><%= login_details %></p>
  <% end %>

  <%# His key is `action` ("Sign In"). portal_button is kept as a fallback because his
      Canada file used that name. %>
  <% button = t.call(:action).presence || t.call(:portal_button) %>
  <% if button.present? %>
    <%= render 'spree/shared/mailer_button', url: @storefront_url, label: button %>
  <% end %>

  <%# shop_args here too. The lambda above got them and this call did not, so the
      bullets kept their placeholders and the welcome email went out with
      href='%{shop_url_full}' as a literal link. Fixing the paragraph and missing
      the list beside it is exactly the shape of the first fix. %>
  <%# His retail files carry a second list the commercial file has no equivalent
  #  for: why_shop_title ("Why you'll love shopping with us") and why_shop_bullets.
  #  The template was written against the commercial vocabulary, so this pair was
  #  read by nothing and never appeared in a single email on any shop. Rendered
  #  above "What's next?" because that is the order he wrote them in the file.
  #  Same alignment handling as the list below, for the same reason. %>
  <% why = email_lines(:why_shop_bullets, scope: @copy_scope, contact_url: @contact_url, note: @note, **shop_args) %>
  <% if why.any? %>
    <% why_title = t.call(:why_shop_title) %>
    <% if why_title.present? %>
      <h2 style="font-size:16px;margin:24px 0 8px;"><%= why_title %></h2>
    <% end %>
    <ul style="margin:0 0 16px;padding-left:20px;text-align:left;">
      <% why.each do |line| %>
        <li style="margin-bottom:6px;text-align:left;"><%= raw line %></li>
      <% end %>
    </ul>
  <% end %>

  <% bullets = email_lines(:what_bullets, scope: @copy_scope, contact_url: @contact_url, note: @note, **shop_args) %>
  <% if bullets.any? %>
    <% title = t.call(:what_next_title) %>
    <% if title.present? %>
      <h2 style="font-size:16px;margin:24px 0 8px;"><%= title %></h2>
    <% end %>
    <%# text-align:left, explicitly.
        The shared mailer hero centres its contents, so the list inherited that:
        the bullet markers stayed at the left edge, where list-style-position
        puts them, and the words centred away from them. On screen it reads as
        dots stranded on the left of centred text. Inline, because an email has
        no stylesheet to lean on, and on the li as well since some clients drop
        inherited alignment on list items. %>
    <ul style="margin:0 0 16px;padding-left:20px;text-align:left;">
      <% bullets.each do |line| %>
        <li style="margin-bottom:6px;text-align:left;"><%= raw line %></li>
      <% end %>
    </ul>
  <% end %>

  <%# Only the not-approved section has this, and it is the operative sentence there: it
      tells them where to reply with what was asked for. %>
  <% contact = t.call(:contact) %>
  <% if contact.present? %>
    <p><%= raw contact %></p>
  <% end %>

  <%# Only the welcome email sets @reset_url, and only when the shop creates accounts
      rather than the customer registering. On this client's shop the apply form collects a
      password, so the flag is off and this block never renders. Kept because a shop whose
      admin creates customers has no other way in. %>
  <% if @reset_url.present? %>
    <%= render 'spree/shared/mailer_button', url: @reset_url,
               label: Spree.t('customer_mailer.welcome_email.cta') %>
    <p class="sub"><%= Spree.t('customer_mailer.welcome_email.expiry') %></p>
    <%# Every mail client eventually eats a button, and this link is the only way in. %>
    <p class="sub">
      <%= Spree.t('customer_mailer.welcome_email.fallback') %>
      <br>
      <span style="word-break: break-all;"><%= @reset_url %></span>
    </p>
  <% end %>

  <% thank_you = t.call(:thank_you) %>
  <% if thank_you.present? %>
    <p><%= thank_you %></p>
  <% end %>

  <% portal_note = t.call(:portal_note,
                          shop_url_full: Spree.t(:shop_url_full, scope: [:user_emails, :common],
                                                 default: @storefront_url)) %>
  <% if portal_note.present? %>
    <p class="sub"><%= portal_note %></p>
  <% end %>

  <%# The founder sign-off, the same block the order, shipping and refund mails carry. %>
  <%# It was missing here, so the 13 Aug reply is wrong in a bigger way than the %>
  <%# slogan being absent: there was no founder line for it to sit under at all. %>
  <%# The slogan renders only where he has supplied the word, which today is the %>
  <%# business shop alone. Every other shop file has owner_name and owner_title %>
  <%# and no slogan, so those mails sign off with the founder and nothing more. %>
  <p class="text-center" style="margin: 24px 0 10px 0;mso-line-height-rule: exactly;text-align: center;">
    <strong><%= Spree.t(:owner_name, scope: [:user_emails, :common]) %></strong><br>
    <em><%= Spree.t(:owner_title, scope: [:user_emails, :common]) %></em>
  </p>
  <% slogan = Spree.t(:slogan, scope: [:user_emails, :common], default: '') %>
  <% if slogan.present? %>
    <p class="text-center" style="margin: 0 0 10px 0;mso-line-height-rule: exactly;text-align: center;"><%= slogan %></p>
  <% end %>
<% end %>
